<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arib al-Ma'muniyya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">797–890</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as ʿArīb al-Ma’mūnīya, Arib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arib al-Ma'muniyya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiyan">qayna</a>, a slave trained in the arts of entertainment, of the early Abbasid period, characterised as the most famous slave singer ever to reside at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> court. Born in 797, she claimed to be the daughter of the Barmakid Ja'far ibn Yahya, a parentage modern scholars have questioned; she was bought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, and was freed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>. A poet, singer, composer and oud player, she left songs said to number around 1,000, and the Kitab al-Aghani of Abu'l-Faraj al-Isfahani places her singing without rival among her peers. Her career spanned the courts of five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, and she died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in 890, aged 96.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arib was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Medieval rumour made her the daughter of the vizier Ja'far al-Barmaki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, one of the family's domestic servants, stolen and sold as a child when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a> fell. She was either born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> or sold into it at about ten after her family's downfall, but she was clearly enslaved for important parts of her early life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">Al-Amin</a> took her as a favourite concubine, and after his death in 813 she passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, whose wife or concubine she became and whose favourite singer she is said to have been. Her own poetry twice protests against her servile status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The main source for her life is the tenth-century Kitāb al-Aghānī of Abū ’l-Faraj al-Iṣfahānī. Like her peers she was trained in poetry, composition and performance, and also in backgammon, chess and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>. Her instrument was the oud, a preference she passed on to her pupils. Citing Ibn al-Mu‘tazz, Abū ’l-Faraj speaks of notebooks and loose sheets holding her songs, and he ranks her, alone among her peers, with the Hijaziyat, the singers of the earliest Islamic period.</p><p>Her surviving verse and the anecdotes about her suggest a number of relationships with male lovers and patrons; one modern scholar writes that she, like many of her peers, was a concubine as well as a singer when circumstances required. After her manumission by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, who reigned from 833 to 842, she seems to have kept a large entourage of her own and to have owned land.</p><p>One of the best-known stories tells of a singing contest in which she and her singing-girls defeated her younger rival Shariyah and her troupe. The picture that emerges is of a woman described as wilful, deeply intelligent, impatient with those of lesser wits, and often cynical. A couplet of hers addresses a faithless lover: &quot;To you treachery is a virtue, you have many faces and ten tongues.&quot;</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arib al-Ma'muniyya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=857" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arib al-Ma'muniyya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=857">What was here in 857 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
